  "difficulty": 2,
  "prompt": "Average monthly temperature in a city is modelled by $T(x) = 4\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{6}\\right) + 7$, where $x$ is the number of months since January. What is the period of this model, in months?",
  "answerType": "numeric",
  "primaryAnswer": "12",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "Period $= \\frac{2\\pi}{B}$, where $B$ is whatever multiplies $x$ inside the cosine.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "Period $= \\frac{2\\pi}{B}$, where $B$ is whatever multiplies $x$ inside the cosine."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "Here $B = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$, so compute $2\\pi \\div \\frac{\\pi}{6}$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$2\\pi \\times \\frac{6}{\\pi} = 12$ months."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "The coefficient of $x$ inside the cosine is $B = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$.",
    "Period $= \\frac{2\\pi}{B} = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\pi/6} = 2\\pi \\times \\frac{6}{\\pi} = 12$.",
    "A period of $12$ months is exactly what you want for a model of the seasons — it repeats once a year."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Sanity-check with the calendar",
      "steps": [
        "At $x = 0$ (January) the model gives $4\\cos(0) + 7 = 11$ degrees.",
        "At $x = 12$ (the next January) it gives $4\\cos(2\\pi) + 7 = 11$ degrees again.",
        "The model has come full circle after $12$ months, so the period is $12$."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tags": [
    "period",
    "modelling",
    "temperature"
  ],
